Q: Issue with HTML to PDF Conversion in Node.js A: <p>In Chrome/Chromium print-to-PDF, a border on an element that spans multiple pages is **not** a page border. The element is fragmented across pages, so the border can be clipped at page edges and content can overlap during page breaks (especially with `position: fixed/absolute` blocks).</p>
<p>So,<br />
Use a fixed frame that repeats on every printed page, and pad the document content away from it.</p>
<pre><code>    @media print {  
      /\* Never use 0 left/right margins if you draw a border \*/  
      @page { size: A4; margin: 12mm; }  

      :root { --frame-border: 3px; --frame-gap: 10mm; }  

      body {  
        margin: 0;  
        padding: 0;  
        background: none;  
        -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;  
        print-color-adjust: exact;  
      }  

      /\* Page border/frame (repeats per page in Chromium) \*/  
      body::before {  
        content: &quot;&quot;;  
        position: fixed;  
        inset: 0;  
        border: var(--frame-border) solid #c9975b;  
        border-radius: 15px;  
        box-sizing: border-box;  
        pointer-events: none;  
      }  

      /\* Keep content away from the frame \*/  
      .container {  
        border: 0 !important;                /\* don't use element border as page border \*/  
        margin: 0 !important;  
        padding: calc(var(--frame-border) + var(--frame-gap)) !important;  
        box-shadow: none !important;  
      }  

      /\* Avoid overlap caused by absolute/fixed blocks inside multipage flow \*/  
      .contact-section,  
      .footer,  
      .footer-bg,  
      .footer-bg_inner,  
      .footer-bg_inner1 {  
        position: static !important;  
        inset: auto !important;  
      }  

      /\* Page breaking \*/  
      .page-break { break-before: page; }  
      .section, .gallery-item-wrapper { break-inside: avoid; }  
      h2 { break-after: avoid; }  

      /\* Optional: avoid grid pagination issues \*/  
      .content { display: block !important; }  
    }  
</code></pre>

Q: What is the use of forwarding references in deduction guides? A: <p><code>ContainerView</code> class requires template parameter  <code>Range</code> to be a <code>view</code>, which contains a <code>view</code> member variable:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;std::ranges::input_range Range&gt;
  requires std::ranges::view&lt;Range&gt;
class ContainerView : public std::ranges::view_interface&lt;ContainerView&lt;Range&gt;&gt;
{
private:
    Range range_{};
    // ...
};
</code></pre>
<p>The deduction guide (use different names to avoid confusion):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename Range2&gt;
ContainerView(Range2&amp;&amp; range) -&gt; ContainerView&lt;std::ranges::views::all_t&lt;Range2&gt;&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>is to convert any arbitrary range <code>Range2</code> to a <code>view</code> using <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/ranges/all_view.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>all_t</code></a> (if possible), which takes into account the ref-qualifier of the range when performing the conversion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is there any benefit that <code>Range</code> is deduced as <code>std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp;</code> instead of <code>std::vector&lt;int&gt;</code>?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is necessary. The deduction guide derives <code>ContainerView(vector&lt;int&gt;&amp;)</code> into <code>ContainerView&lt;views::all_t&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&amp;&gt;&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>Since <code>vector&lt;int&gt;&amp;</code> is an <em>lvalue</em>, it can be wrapped by <code>ref_view</code> and thus become a <code>view</code>, ultimately resulting in <code>ContainerView&lt;ref_view&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;&gt;
class ContainerView&lt;ref_view&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;&gt; : public view_interface&lt;...&gt;
{
private:
    ref_view&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; range_{};
    // ...
public:
    ContainerView() = default;
    constexpr ContainerView(ref_view&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; r);
    // ...
};
</code></pre>
<p>This ensures that the <code>ContainerView</code> internally stores a reference to a <code>vector&lt;int&gt;</code> instead of owning the data, which meets the complexity requirements of a <code>view</code>, i.e., cheap to copy:</p>
<pre><code>std::vector&lt;int&gt; vec;
ContainerView view{vec}; // ContainerView&lt;ref_view&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;&gt;, which is a view
</code></pre>

Q: How do you filter multiple related records using prisma? A: <p>Prisma supports this natively using <strong>relation filters</strong> like <code>some</code>, <code>every</code>, and <code>none</code>.</p>
<pre><code>const users = await prisma.user.findMany({
  where: {
    posts: {
      some: {
        status: 'PUBLISHED'
      }
    }
  }
});
</code></pre>
<p>You can also use:</p>
<pre><code>posts: { every: { status: 'PUBLISHED' } }
posts: { none: { status: 'DRAFT' } }
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>For dynamic APIs (query params)</h3>
<p>If relation filters come from request query parameters, manually building <code>where</code> conditions becomes repetitive. You can centralize this logic using <strong>prisma-qb</strong>, which generates Prisma <code>where</code> objects from validated query params:</p>
<pre><code>import { buildPrismaQuery } from &quot;prisma-qb&quot;;

const { where } = buildPrismaQuery({
  query: req.query,
  filterFields: [
    { key: &quot;postStatus&quot;, field: &quot;posts.status&quot;, operator: &quot;some&quot; }
  ]
});

await prisma.user.findMany({ where });
</code></pre>
<p>Package: <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/prisma-qb" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.npmjs.com/package/prisma-qb</a><br />
Ecosystem: <a href="https://www.prisma.io/ecosystem" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.prisma.io/ecosystem</a></p>
